<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#kannada">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kannada</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kannada language</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kannada is a Dravidian language spoken mainly in the Indian state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a>, with more than 40 million speakers. Its earliest known inscription, at Halmidi, dates from around 450, and it developed a literature from the 9th century under the Rashtrakutas and later dynasties. It is written in the Kannada script, closely related to that of Telugu, and is the official language of Karnataka.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kannada.jpg" alt="Kannada" width="320" height="190" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rishu Shukla · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Shukla_Kannada.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The great majority of subjects were Hindu peasants speaking <b>Kannada</b> and Marathi, and the countryside was administered through hereditary local officials, the Marathi-speaking deshmukhs and deshpandes, whose cooperation the sultans needed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Sanskrit was the language of court poetry and of most royal charters, while <b>Kannada</b> appeared increasingly in inscriptions, including the verse inscription of Kappe Arabhatta at Badami, usually dated to about 700 and counted among the earliest examples of Kannada poetry.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chalukyas-of-badami">Chalukyas of Badami · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal revenue came from land, trade and tolls, recorded in <b>Kannada</b> inscriptions that are among the most numerous of any medieval Indian kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The court, nobility and much of the administration were Muslim and Urdu-speaking, while over four-fifths of the population was Hindu and spoke Telugu, Marathi or <b>Kannada</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hyderabad-state">Hyderabad State · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Kannada</b> literature flourished at the court of Chikka Devaraja Wodeyar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Chalukyas recovered under Vikramaditya I, and around 740 Vikramaditya II occupied Kanchipuram, though he spared its temples and left a <b>Kannada</b> inscription in the Kailasanatha temple recording his gifts.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Military</a></li></ul></section>
